==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abaissement}}, from {{der|en|frm|abaissement|t=astonishment}}. Equivalent to {{suffix|en|abase|ment}}. Compare French ''[[abaissement]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbeɪs.mənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abasement.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪsmənt|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[abasing]], [[humbling]], or bringing low. {{defdate|Mid 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=Chambers>{{R:CDOE|page=2}}.</ref><ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=2}}</ref>
# The state of being abased or [[humbled]]; [[humiliation]]. {{defdate|Mid 16<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=Chambers/><ref name=SOED/>
#* {{RQ:Hollinghurst Line|chapter=17|passage=He was wearing cavalry twill trousers and a buff crew-neck sweater. The effect was of symbolic '''abasement''' mixed with military resolve {{...}}}}

